CONTRIBUTION_TYPE: insight
SCALE_REALISM: "Partial. The combinatorial length-bias mechanism holds for any remove-on-any-kgram-overlap filter under nonzero per-position match probability, and OSS implementations do use that any-match structure. But the packet evidence is L0 synthetic: iid per-kgram q, synthetic length distribution/difficulty, no real web corpus n-gram index, no real benchmark item lengths, and no measured per-item accuracy slope. Realistic-scale reproducibility is not yet shown; L1 should run lm-eval/DataTrove-style decontamination against C4/Pile/web n-grams on GSM8K/QuALITY/MMLU and report survivor length shift plus realized score delta."
RATIONALE: "This is not mere glue: the contribution is the selection-effect insight that an already-shipping any-match n-gram decontamination mechanism acts as a length-biased sampler, with score inflation only when difficulty is length-coupled. Existing systems already implement the underlying mechanism: lm-evaluation-harness says contamination detection builds eval n-gram dictionaries, scans training n-grams, marks matches contaminated, and evaluator excludes contaminated documents; DataTrove's NGramsDecontFilter removes a document when any computed n-gram hash appears in the eval-task index. Prior work cited in packet/Bordt 2410.03249 appears close because it applies filtering and observes filtering, but the packet claims the missing delta is that collateral removal is not benign when length correlates with difficulty. Vote is yellow because the systems-level mechanism is real and reproducible at toy scale, but realistic workload validity is still insufficient evidence: q is modeled, not measured; benchmark difficulty coupling is asserted but not measured here; and leaderboard-level impact remains unproven."
